---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// The address the Connect IQ Store listing points at, and the one people will
// actually write to. It has to be a mailbox that exists — a privacy contact
// that bounces is worse than none at all.
const CONTACT = 'privacy@scottlovegrove.co.uk';
const COMPANION_URL = 'https://sparmin-app.scottlovegrove.co.uk';

const updated = '2 August 2026';
---

<Base
    title="Sparmin — privacy"
    description="What Sparmin collects, when, and how to delete it. The watch app sends nothing until you link it to an account."
>
    <section class="intro">
        <h1>Privacy</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            The watch app sends nothing anywhere until you deliberately link it to an account. If
            you never link it, nothing on this page applies to you.
        </p>
        <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy">
        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <p>
            Sparmin records your spa visit on your watch and saves it to Garmin Connect, exactly
            like any other activity. That much involves nobody but you and Garmin.
        </p>
        <p>
            There is also an optional companion web app. If you choose to link your watch to it,
            each finished session is sent there so you can look at your history in a browser.
            That is the only thing that leaves your watch, it only happens after you link, and you
            can stop it at any time.
        </p>

        <h2>What is sent, if you link</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>When each session started and ended, and your time zone offset.</li>
            <li>
                How long you spent at each station, how many times you visited it, and its name —
                “Finnish sauna”, “Ice cave”, and so on.
            </li>
            <li>
                Your average, maximum and minimum heart rate for each station. This is health
                data, which is why linking is a deliberate opt-in rather than something switched
                on for you.
            </li>
            <li>
                An identifier for the watch, and the app version — so a session can be attributed
                to the right device, and a problem traced to the right release.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What is never sent</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Your location.</strong> Sparmin does not record GPS at all, so there is none
                to send.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Your name or email address.</strong> The watch has no access to them.
            </li>
            <li><strong>The activity file itself.</strong> That stays on your watch and in Garmin Connect.</li>
            <li><strong>Your watch's serial number.</strong></li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Signing in to the companion</h2>
        <p>
            The companion needs an email address so it can sign you in, by magic link or passkey.
            That address is used for signing in and nothing else: no marketing, no newsletters, and
            it is not shared with anyone.
        </p>

        <h2>Where it is kept</h2>
        <p>
            On Cloudflare's platform, in a database used only by Sparmin. Email delivery for sign-in
            links goes through <a href="https://resend.com">Resend</a>. Nothing is sold, shared or
            handed to advertisers, and there is no analytics or tracking in the watch app or the
            companion.
        </p>
        <p>
            Sparmin is run by Scott Lovegrove as a personal project, not a company. It is
            <a href="https://github.com/scottlovegrove/sparmin">open source</a>, so you can read
            exactly what it does with your data rather than take this page's word for it.
        </p>

        <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
        <p>
            Your sessions are kept until you delete them. There is no automatic expiry — a spa
            history is only useful over years, so deleting it on your behalf would defeat the point.
        </p>

        <h2>Deleting it</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>One session:</strong> delete it from the session list in the
                <a href={COMPANION_URL}>companion</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Everything:</strong> Settings → Delete account. This removes your account
                and every session with it, immediately and permanently.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Stop sending, keep your history:</strong> Settings → Watches → Remove. The
                watch notices, forgets the account and stops sending. What it already sent stays
                until you delete it.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Deleting your account does not touch Garmin Connect. Your activities live there
            independently, and Sparmin has never had access to that account.
        </p>

        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>
            Under UK and EU data protection law you can ask for a copy of your data, ask for it to
            be corrected, or ask for it to be deleted. The delete route above does the last one
            immediately and without asking anybody. For the others, write to
            <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The lawful basis for handling heart-rate data is your explicit consent, given by
            linking a watch, and withdrawn by removing it or deleting your account.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>
            If this policy changes in a way that affects what is collected, the change will appear
            in the <a href="/changelog">changelog</a> alongside the release that caused it.
        </p>
    </section>
</Base>

<style>
    .intro {
        border-top: 0;
        padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 4rem) 0;
    }

    .updated {
        color: var(--muted, #6b7280);
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        margin-block-start: 0.75rem;
    }

    .policy {
        padding-block: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2.5rem) clamp(3rem, 8vw, 5rem);
        max-width: 46rem;
    }

    .policy h2 {
        margin-block: 2rem 0.75rem;
    }

    .policy h2:first-of-type {
        margin-block-start: 0;
    }

    .policy ul {
        padding-inline-start: 1.25rem;
    }

    .policy li {
        margin-block: 0.4rem;
    }
</style>
